{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key noble in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. His early rule was marked by a regency led by his mother and later by his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond was known for his cautious and diplomatic nature which often put him at odds with more aggressive nobles. He spent much of his youth under the shadow of powerful figures and struggled to assert his independence. His reign began in a time of growing tension between the Crusader states and the Muslim powers led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures which gave him a claim to the throne of Jerusalem through his wife. He was named regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III and the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was chosen as regent due to his experience and status as the most powerful noble in the region. His regency was troubled by internal rivalries especially with the rising power of Guy of Lusignan and the military orders. Raymond was often accused of being too cautious and unwilling to confront Saladin directly. He was briefly deposed as regent in 1176 when the nobles chose Raymond of Antioch instead but was later restored after political shifts.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was instrumental in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash decision to march to Tiberias against Saladin. He argued for a defensive strategy but was overruled by Guy of Lusignan and the Knights Templar. 